<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-product-coproduct" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Product and Coproduct</title>

  <p> 	In this section we define products and coproducts of sets as well as topological spaces. </p>
<definition xml:id="def-product-of-sets">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty set and consider sets <m>X_\alpha</m> for every <m>\alpha\in I</m>. The \emph{product of <m>X_\alpha</m>} is a set denoted by <m>\prod X_\alpha</m> and it is `characterized'\footnote{see Exercise \ref{product-uniqueness} for the precise meaning} by the following properties.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          For each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, there is a map <m>\pi_\alpha\colon\prod X_\alpha\to X_\alpha</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>Y</m> is any set together with maps <m>f_\alpha\colon Y\to X_\alpha</m> for each <m>\alpha</m>, then there is a unique map <m>g\colon Y\to\prod X_\alpha</m> satisfying <m>\pi_\alpha\circ g=f_\alpha</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      These conditions can be summarize in the following `diagram'.
    </p>
    
  </statement>
</definition>
<p>
  <figure xml:id="fig-tikz">
  <caption>A figure generated with TikZ in
  <latex /></caption>
  <image width="15%" xml:id="poset">

<latex-image >
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw (0,0) -- (0,1);
\draw (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (1,2) -- (1,3);
\draw (0,0) -- (1,2);
\draw (0,1) -- (1,0);
\draw [fill] (0,0) circle [radius=0.1];
\draw [fill] (0,1) circle [radius=0.1];
\draw [fill] (1,0) circle [radius=0.1];
\draw [fill] (1,1) circle [radius=0.1];
\draw [fill] (1,2) circle [radius=0.1];
\draw [fill] (1,3) circle [radius=0.1];
\node [left] at (0,0) {$x$};
\node [left] at (0,1) {$b$};
\node [right] at (1,0) {$a$};
\node [right] at (1,1) {$y$};
\node [right] at (1,2) {$c$};
\node [right] at (1,3) {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
  </image>

</figure>
</p>
</section>